1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
300 mile per hour fire belching monsters.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Computers and space age composites.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Now, race cars on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,000
And he's over!

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Whether it's screaming around a two-mile oval track

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
or thundering down a quarter-mile strip at 320 miles per hour,

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
fans love the speed, excitement, and danger of racing.

8
00:00:56,001 --> 00:01:00,500
There are many types of automobile racing.

9
00:01:00,501 --> 00:01:07,260
America's favorite are drag, stock car, and open wheel racing.

10
00:01:07,261 --> 00:01:10,440
Each type employs different machines.

11
00:01:10,441 --> 00:01:16,760
Aerodynamically tested, supercharged, reinforced, earth-shaking vehicles.

12
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:24,000
They're fast, they're thrilling, and they employ the highest of high tech.

13
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:32,000
In America, the IndyCar and internationally the Formula One car are the most popular open

14
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:33,000
wheel race vehicles.

15
00:01:33,001 --> 00:01:39,000
To the casual observer, they may look the same, but in truth, they're quite different.

16
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:44,000
A Formula One car is capable of doing a variety of things.

17
00:01:44,001 --> 00:01:49,000
You can stop at zero and then accelerate to 180, go through a tunnel, take a sharp left,

18
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:52,000
take a sharp right, and they're probably the best handling cars in the world.

19
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:54,200
Well, oval or Indy racing is a little bit different.

20
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:59,360
The word oval obviously means you're racing at very high speeds around oval-shaped circuits,

21
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:01,700
where you're prime concerned with speed.

22
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:03,640
In an oval, you can achieve perfection.

23
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:11,560
The most famous oval track in the world is the Indianapolis Motor Speedway,

24
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:19,060
America's arena, where wheeled gladiators go head-to-head in a 500-mile battle for supremacy.

25
00:02:19,061 --> 00:02:28,060
Drawing more than 300,000 people, the Indianapolis 500 is the largest single-day sporting event in the world.

26
00:02:28,061 --> 00:02:39,060
Whether it's an IndyCar or a Formula One, each traces its common ancestry back to the late 1800s and the first automobile race.

27
00:02:39,061 --> 00:02:39,761
race.

28
00:02:39,762 --> 00:02:49,500
The first competition ever was in France in 1894 and it was a reliability run and the idea

29
00:02:49,501 --> 00:02:54,960
was just to prove the the worth of the automobile and the fact that it could actually make a trip.

30
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:01,040
For this early endurance race of 80 miles, the winning car averaged a breakneck speed

31
00:03:01,041 --> 00:03:07,920
of 11.6 miles per hour. Competing that day were also several steam-driven vehicles,

32
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:13,580
but they were no match for the cars using petroleum-powered internal combustion engines.

33
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,660
Those internal combustion engines worked basically the same as they do today.

34
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:25,780
Internal combustion occurs when the compressed fuel mixture is ignited inside the cylinders.

35
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:29,520
Thermal energy is converted into mechanical energy

36
00:03:29,521 --> 00:03:33,160
when the force from these explosions thrust the pistons down,

37
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,260
causing the crankshaft to rotate.

38
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:42,260
This rotating force is transmitted through the drive train to the wheels and propels the vehicle forward.

39
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:53,260
Crowds loved the 1894 exhibition and it didn't take long for auto racing to become an international phenomenon.

40
00:03:53,261 --> 00:04:03,260
In America, beginning in the late 1800s, promoters held races on road courses, dirt tracks and specially built ovals.

41
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:08,080
Crowds flocked to the events where racers risked life and limb to finish first

42
00:04:08,081 --> 00:04:10,660
in the now high-speed spectacles.

43
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,680
The cars had developed by leaps and bounds.

44
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:22,780
I mean, they'd gone from 4, 5, 6 miles an hour to speeds topping 70, 75.

45
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:26,140
The increase in technology was very rapid.

46
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:32,040
By the turn of the century, speeds had increased by a factor of 10.

47
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:39,560
Hundreds of car companies existed in America, and a large number around Indianapolis, Indiana.

48
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:46,300
Indianapolis Motor Speedway was really the vision of one man, Carl Fisher,

49
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:55,540
who at the time was an Indianapolis businessman and was involved in the automobile industry,

50
00:04:56,220 --> 00:04:58,540
a proving ground for the automobile industry.

51
00:04:58,541 --> 00:05:03,440
That's how he really visioned the Indianapolis Motor Speedway.

52
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:10,600
In 1909, Carl Fisher built the famous 2.5-mile track to road test new car models.

53
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:16,020
Also, several times during the year, automobile races would be conducted.

54
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:23,300
The manufacturers and the dealers would have an opportunity to put into competition what they were trying to sell.

55
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,880
A lot of people didn't own a car yet, and they're looking at this thing with great suspicion.

56
00:05:26,881 --> 00:05:31,500
So you sit up in the grandstands and watch the races and figure out which would be the best thing to buy.

57
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:37,280
In 1911, the track hosted the first Indianapolis 500-mile race,

58
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:40,800
competing with the ancestors of the modern IndyCar.

59
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:47,460
They were actually passenger cars entered by the factories or the dealers and stripped down.

60
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:51,000
But there was about seven or eight in there weren't.

61
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:54,140
They were built for competition by the manufacturers.

62
00:05:54,141 --> 00:06:00,480
The cars ran heavy four- and six-cylinder motors producing only about 100 horsepower.

63
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:05,380
To keep an eye on gauges and other cars attempting to pass,

64
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:08,240
each car also carried a riding mechanic.

65
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:10,240
All but one.

66
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:16,520
Ray Haroon, who worked for Marmon and was a driver, but he was also very much of an engineer,

67
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:20,260
and they figured that they could save the weight of a second person

68
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:23,360
by building a single-seat racing car,

69
00:06:23,361 --> 00:06:29,520
and that they would centrally locate the body and, you know, put streamlining on it and everything else.

70
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:31,520
And that's what won the race.

71
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:36,940
Ray Haroon won that year in his 7.8-liter six-cylinder Marmon Wasp.

72
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:41,920
Cars like his, which were specifically built for racing, proved superior.

73
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:51,800
There was a very quick end to stripping down passenger cars or even racing with production parts.

74
00:06:51,801 --> 00:06:57,380
It wasn't long before they started to build 100% race cars.

75
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:06,100
By the 1920s, streamlined, specially crafted cars replaced the top-heavy, stripped production models of the previous decade.

76
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:13,540
Most historians refer to the 1920s as the golden age of the American racing car.

77
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:20,260
And Harry Miller is their hero because he built complete racing cars.

78
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:24,360
and they were just beautifully handcrafted.

79
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:28,140
I've had people tell me that they would grind a camshaft

80
00:07:28,141 --> 00:07:33,200
and maybe have, you know, 40, 50 hours in it.

81
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,100
And then he would decide, no, this has got a floor in it,

82
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:37,540
let's throw it away and start again.

83
00:07:37,541 --> 00:07:42,280
He took it to extremes by just building these works of art.

84
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:45,460
The Miller engine was extremely successful

85
00:07:45,461 --> 00:07:48,420
and defined Indy racing in the 1920s.

86
00:07:48,421 --> 00:07:53,420
But new regulations and the depression altered the 1930s IndyCar.

87
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:56,420
Race cars took a U-turn.

88
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:00,420
To encourage participation and lower the cost of building cars,

89
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:04,420
new rules permitted more traditional production vehicles.

90
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:07,420
The last lap tires way ahead of the field.

91
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:09,420
Here he comes around the curve, into the set.

92
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:14,420
Most cars in the early 1930s were again simply modified passenger cars,

93
00:08:14,421 --> 00:08:19,860
purchased at the local dealership and stripped of fenders, windshields, bumpers and running boards.

94
00:08:19,861 --> 00:08:27,600
One of the biggest stars in the 1930s was Louis Meyer, the first three-time Indy 500 winner.

95
00:08:27,601 --> 00:08:31,681
Meyer raced in a hazardous environment.

96
00:08:31,682 --> 00:08:36,600
No seat belts, no roll bar, no head protection.

97
00:08:36,601 --> 00:08:44,360
When my father drove, the drivers wore a cloth helmet and it was a white cloth helmet usually.

98
00:08:44,361 --> 00:08:47,660
and that wasn't to keep the hair out of their eyes.

99
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:51,300
If they went to a wreck and the helmet was red,

100
00:08:51,380 --> 00:08:53,720
they know that there was skull damage.

101
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:58,700
Racing has always been an inherently dangerous sport

102
00:08:58,701 --> 00:09:02,260
where both car and driver are pushed to the limit.

103
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:07,000
As speeds increased to well over 100 miles per hour,

104
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:11,380
so did serious accidents and the demand for better safety measures.

105
00:09:11,381 --> 00:09:18,380
My father, he was in third and he was trying to catch the leaders and he came off the second turn and he just lost it.

106
00:09:18,381 --> 00:09:22,380
And he hit the inside fence, the car spun once, it flew up on its side.

107
00:09:22,381 --> 00:09:29,380
He didn't have seat belts then and he was thrown out in the race track, he slid out onto the track, he jumped up, ran into the infield.

108
00:09:29,381 --> 00:09:31,938
He was taken to the hospital, he didn't have his shoes.

109
00:09:31,939 --> 00:09:35,380
When they got the car back to the garage, his shoes were still in the car.

110
00:09:35,381 --> 00:09:36,081
in the car.

111
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:37,460
Too late, he's into the rail.

112
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:39,380
A plunging car hurls him out onto the track.

113
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:41,400
Other cars skim perilously close.

114
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:42,780
But Meyer escapes on late.

115
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:44,940
Meyer was lucky to survive.

116
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:48,460
Eventually, officials began mandating seat belts,

117
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:50,940
protective clothing, helmets, and roll bars.

118
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:53,560
Even the cars became safer.

119
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:58,620
In the 50s, it was the Roadster, which was all iron

120
00:09:58,621 --> 00:10:04,780
and, you know, just strong tank-like, like, like automobiles.

121
00:10:04,781 --> 00:10:09,380
and when they'd hit the wall, all of the impact would go straight to the driver.

122
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:17,720
And so then in the 70s, they started figuring out that if you hit the wall and pieces start flying off,

123
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:23,040
that that in itself is absorbing the impact rather than the driver.

124
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:28,160
Cars continued to evolve over the next several decades.

125
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:32,940
Designers dropped the cars closer to the ground for a lower center of gravity.

126
00:10:32,941 --> 00:10:41,940
tires became wider engineers moved the engine from in front of the driver to behind and better motors

127
00:10:41,941 --> 00:10:50,940
pushed cars faster and faster connecting rods crank shafts combustion chambers one man who

128
00:10:50,941 --> 00:10:57,360
has seen the evolution of the IndyCar firsthand is Louie Meyer jr he's been working on race car

129
00:10:57,361 --> 00:10:59,633
engines for more than 50 years.

130
00:10:59,634 --> 00:11:04,180
There's a constant development in racing engines and materials

131
00:11:04,181 --> 00:11:07,057
and camshafts and valve springs.

132
00:11:07,058 --> 00:11:12,720
It's just taking weight out of the parts and lightening them up

133
00:11:12,721 --> 00:11:14,580
and raising the RPM of the engine.

134
00:11:14,581 --> 00:11:17,480
From the beginning of Indianapolis for those old cast

135
00:11:17,481 --> 00:11:25,600
iron buggies and wooden wheels and you know the way they ran them to the modern day race car is

136
00:11:25,601 --> 00:11:27,440
is a tremendous gulf.

137
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:33,140
Then the wings came along and started pinning the cars down

138
00:11:33,141 --> 00:11:35,640
with aerodynamics, the wide, low-profile tires,

139
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:38,420
all of the technical changes that came along

140
00:11:38,421 --> 00:11:40,600
to make the cars more stable at high speeds,

141
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:44,620
and now we see them going around at 200 miles an hour just at leisure.

142
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:50,420
Aerodynamics and better engines produced faster cars.

143
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,400
Some say too fast.

144
00:11:52,401 --> 00:12:01,600
Next, can a driver survive when his car traveling a quarter of an inch off the ground hits the wall at over 200 miles per hour?

145
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:10,400
Ray Haroon's Marmon Wasp won the first Indy 500, averaging 74.61 miles per hour.

146
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:13,400
Race cars will return on Modern Marvels.

147
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:20,400
We now return to race cars on Modern Marvels.

148
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:30,400
Today's IndyCars are computer designed, wind tunnel tested, hand assembled technological

149
00:12:30,401 --> 00:12:31,400
wonders.

150
00:12:31,401 --> 00:12:36,400
A modern car begins with a chassis or tub.

151
00:12:36,401 --> 00:12:41,400
It's the compartment where the driver sits and is considered the survival cell in case

152
00:12:41,401 --> 00:12:42,400
of a crash.

153
00:12:42,401 --> 00:12:47,400
You might see a crash in Indianapolis where a car hits the wall and it shatters into

154
00:12:47,401 --> 00:12:50,400
a thousand pieces and you'll see very small pieces.

155
00:12:50,401 --> 00:12:57,120
well that's what you want to see in an auto accident because it dissipates all the energy

156
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:07,360
new building materials make cars lighter stronger and safer

157
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:14,560
here at riley and scott manufacturers use carbon fiber composite materials to build

158
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:19,880
car chassis from scratch. Carbon is a weave and the strength of the carbon

159
00:13:19,881 --> 00:13:26,200
depends on the orientation of the weave. Each little strand of carbon really has

160
00:13:26,201 --> 00:13:30,640
the strength of steel so if you orientate it properly you can actually

161
00:13:30,641 --> 00:13:38,960
get the strength of steel. Fabricators pattern cut carbon fiber. They then place

162
00:13:38,961 --> 00:13:43,240
layers of the weave into a mold where they add a layer of aluminum honeycomb

163
00:13:43,241 --> 00:13:45,600
and top layers of carbon fiber.

164
00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:50,180
The sandwiched honeycomb and carbon fiber is baked,

165
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:53,180
producing an extremely light and durable car chassis,

166
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:55,820
one quarter the weight of steel,

167
00:13:56,340 --> 00:13:58,140
and four times as strong.

168
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:03,480
Cars have gone from weighing 2,500 pounds 75 years ago

169
00:14:03,481 --> 00:14:06,180
to 1,600 pounds today,

170
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:08,300
while speeds have doubled.

171
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:12,520
The chassis is the backbone and critical component

172
00:14:12,521 --> 00:14:13,743
of the race car.

173
00:14:13,744 --> 00:14:19,400
Unlike a normal car chassis which cradles the engine, an Indy car uses the

174
00:14:19,401 --> 00:14:23,620
carbon fiber tub and engine together to support the vehicle.

175
00:14:23,621 --> 00:14:25,800
Basically you have the tub here

176
00:14:25,801 --> 00:14:28,322
and the engine bolts onto the back of it.

177
00:14:28,323 --> 00:14:31,520
The engine is actually a stressed member of the car,

178
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:37,000
meaning that you bolt the suspension, everything, it actually hangs off the motor.

179
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:42,640
We have four basic pieces of the race car, four big lumps if you like.

180
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:49,640
We have the carbon tub, we have the engine here, the bell housing here and the gearbox there.

181
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:54,240
The power from the engine is delivered outboard up here, straight through here and drives the rear wheels at each side.

182
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:59,200
The chassis' aerodynamics is extremely important in racing.

183
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:02,660
The shape of the car determines just how fast it will run

184
00:15:02,661 --> 00:15:07,480
and whether it will adhere to the track while taking a corner or fly over the wall.

185
00:15:08,620 --> 00:15:15,980
At Riley & Scott, designers work to gain an edge over other chassis manufacturers by reducing drag, making cars faster.

186
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,500
But their main goal is to produce more downforce,

187
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:26,740
or the downward pressure created by the moving car that pushes it to the track instead of launching it into the air.

188
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:34,560
The downforce from a modern-day car, about 60% of it is from the overall car body shape,

189
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:39,880
and the rest, like the rest of the 40%, comes from a balance between the front and rear wing.

190
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:45,240
What we're looking at here is what's called the underwing,

191
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:53,020
and it is part of the overall car that contributes 60% of the total downforce to the vehicle.

192
00:15:53,021 --> 00:15:57,580
the underwing bolts onto the bottom of the engine and chassis.

193
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,840
Really, the car itself is a giant wing.

194
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:06,760
Working similar to an inverted airplane wing,

195
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:11,140
air flowing over the car travels slower than the air traveling underneath,

196
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:14,540
creating a low-pressure zone under the vehicle.

197
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:19,880
This produces downforce that literally sucks the car to the track.

198
00:16:19,881 --> 00:16:26,980
Although aerodynamics are important, without a powerhouse propelling the chassis faster

199
00:16:26,981 --> 00:16:31,280
than 200 miles per hour, racing would be a pretty boring sport.

200
00:16:31,281 --> 00:16:39,840
In Indianapolis, Louis Meyer III carries on the family tradition of building racing engines.

201
00:16:39,841 --> 00:16:42,840
We buy the engine in pieces.

202
00:16:42,841 --> 00:16:46,860
And to keep a performance edge in our competition,

203
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:50,020
I buy the cylinder block, the cylinder heads,

204
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:52,740
and all the exterior covers from General Motors.

205
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:57,280
Then all my internal pieces are made by different vendors all over the world.

206
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:01,840
After assembling the engine, Meijer puts it in a dynamometer for testing.

207
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:05,580
The dyno computer simulates racing conditions.

208
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:09,280
We have a state-of-the-art dynamometer.

209
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,620
It's something that nowadays, as competitive as the sport is,

210
00:17:12,621 --> 00:17:15,340
you have to have it or you can't run up front.

211
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:23,440
Both Sonny and Butch Myers strive to run up front, but for different teams.

212
00:17:24,020 --> 00:17:27,220
It's a lot of fun competing against my son.

213
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:31,060
It's a lot of fun competing with him, and it's a lot of fun competing against him.

214
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:34,420
Working on these engines, it's a labor of love.

215
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:39,100
You want to put an engine together, you want to go to the racetrack,

216
00:17:39,160 --> 00:17:40,900
and you want it to run up front.

217
00:17:40,901 --> 00:17:45,400
really the payback you get is the driver winning the race.

218
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,620
Each race team has its own shop where all the pieces finally come together.

219
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:05,300
Chassis, engine, side pods, engine cover, and wings making a race car.

220
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:09,200
To an average guy, it would be like going to the model shop

221
00:18:09,201 --> 00:18:13,080
and buying the biggest, most pieces of a model you can find

222
00:18:13,081 --> 00:18:15,200
and take it home and put it together.

223
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:16,440
You sand every part.

224
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:19,740
You mess with every little piece to make it fit just right.

225
00:18:19,860 --> 00:18:21,860
By the time we put our manpower into it

226
00:18:21,861 --> 00:18:23,820
and change all the parts we want to change,

227
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:29,260
each car that we own is probably worth $700,000 to $750,000

228
00:18:29,261 --> 00:18:31,040
when it has a motor in it.

229
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:34,720
Each team travels to the race with a rolling shop,

230
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:37,600
complete with everything needed to make repairs

231
00:18:37,601 --> 00:18:38,920
and last-minute adjustments.

232
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:49,040
Amazingly, in as little as one and a half hours, mechanics switch out $100,000, 1,000

233
00:18:49,041 --> 00:18:55,720
horsepower engines between every practice, qualifying round, and race to ensure the best

234
00:18:55,721 --> 00:18:56,421
performance.

235
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:03,680
At the track before the race, every car is subjected to at least three full technical

236
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:04,381
inspections.

237
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:06,280
Up.

238
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:07,820
There you go.

239
00:19:07,821 --> 00:19:14,820
In about four minutes, inspectors make more than 200 aerodynamic and safety checks.

240
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:21,420
The inspectors are responsible for keeping the race as fair as possible.

241
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:28,640
The slightest variation in wing angle, car height, or body width could give a team a decisive advantage.

242
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:31,720
And everyone is looking for an advantage.

243
00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:34,340
They will always try and stress the rules.

244
00:19:34,341 --> 00:19:36,340
If you've got a gray area, they'll take care of it.

245
00:19:36,341 --> 00:19:43,700
But if you keep those cars all within a level playing field, then the teams and the drivers, that's where all the competition comes to.

246
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:50,400
Fuel inspectors also keep things equal by taking samples before and after the race.

247
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:52,540
Each team is given methanol.

248
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:56,760
Now these fuels are provided from one source.

249
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:05,780
This is an analytical instrument that can take apart a mixture, separate it, tell me what's in the mixture, and tell me how much is in the mixture.

250
00:20:05,781 --> 00:20:10,160
Generally, the teams are following the guidelines set out by the IRL,

251
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:14,320
but this is just an insurance that they're still following those rules.

252
00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:19,340
But the evolution of the race car is not limited to the engine and chassis.

253
00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:24,920
Firestone tires were on the first Indy 500 winner's car in 1911.

254
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:28,760
The tires were very narrow consumer models.

255
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:35,180
In 1925, the company introduced their first balloon tire, made specifically for racing.

256
00:20:35,780 --> 00:20:39,680
These tires were wider and conformed to the track better.

257
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:43,680
Speeds immediately increased 2 to 3 miles per hour.

258
00:20:46,540 --> 00:20:50,960
Today, Firestone will spend thousands of hours designing a new racing tire.

259
00:20:51,680 --> 00:20:54,200
Primarily two elements that we're concerned with.

260
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:57,260
One is the construction that's the actual carcass of the tire.

261
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:01,440
The second is the compound, the materials that the tire is made out of.

262
00:21:01,441 --> 00:21:08,340
We have compounders that build these tires, and they build in different elements to provide enhanced grip or enhanced durability.

263
00:21:09,060 --> 00:21:13,720
And to match those compounds to a particular track and to a particular weather condition,

264
00:21:14,220 --> 00:21:16,920
you have to do a little bit of cooking there in the lab.

265
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:22,000
Computers also play a vital role in the modern IndyCar.

266
00:21:23,260 --> 00:21:30,120
With over 50 sensors on the car, real-time information is transmitted to the pits while the car is on the track.

267
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:41,996
It tells us how high the car is off the ground, all four tire pressures, all the engine information with, you know, water temperature, oil pressure, oil temperature,

268
00:21:41,997 --> 00:21:43,020
battery voltage.

269
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:45,440
It tells us how much the shocks are moving.

270
00:21:45,760 --> 00:21:51,460
It gives the engineer quite a bit more information that he can go back and look at.

271
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,220
Information is then fed from the pits back to the driver.

272
00:21:56,221 --> 00:22:04,278
And this particular steering wheel that we have here right now, it gives up all the LED information as far as RPMs are concerned, the speed that you're going, your

273
00:22:04,279 --> 00:22:06,700
elapsed time, all the different engine functions.

274
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:12,216
And we can pull up anything at any given time at any point in the racetrack if we feel we want to know any of this information.

275
00:22:12,217 --> 00:22:13,280
It's really a neat system.

276
00:22:15,060 --> 00:22:18,540
Computers have affected the sport of racing more than any single component.

277
00:22:18,541 --> 00:22:22,420
But the driver's still a vital part of it because he's driving the car.

278
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:27,480
And no matter what the electronics tell you, you always have to believe what he tells you

279
00:22:27,481 --> 00:22:29,960
because he's the guy that's connected to the car.

280
00:22:34,540 --> 00:22:38,420
The sport wouldn't be as great without the personalities behind the wheel.

281
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:43,720
Legends like Al Unser and A.J. Foyt, who found Indy immortality.

282
00:22:43,721 --> 00:22:49,800
and a younger generation of drivers like Eddie Cheever, Al Unser Jr., and Scott Goodyear,

283
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:52,520
whose competitive nature drives the sport today.

284
00:22:53,480 --> 00:22:57,360
The neat thing for a driver is that we have an opportunity to drive the car faster

285
00:22:57,361 --> 00:22:58,680
because we all love to go fast.

286
00:22:58,940 --> 00:23:03,220
We all want to win, and if we can make the car really exceed its limit a little bit,

287
00:23:03,260 --> 00:23:04,520
that's what we're paid to do.

288
00:23:04,820 --> 00:23:06,160
And it's really a neat thing for me.

289
00:23:06,161 --> 00:23:09,440
It's a natural high where we can actually put all this engineering together

290
00:23:09,441 --> 00:23:11,740
and all this development that we've done and put it on the racetrack

291
00:23:11,741 --> 00:23:12,960
and go faster than anybody else.

292
00:23:13,720 --> 00:23:22,420
Next, one of the fastest-growing sports in America, Indy's stock car cousins make an impact.

293
00:23:22,421 --> 00:23:35,060
Indy cars driving over 200 miles per hour produce so much downforce that they are capable of traveling upside down on a ceiling without falling back to earth.

294
00:23:35,920 --> 00:23:38,620
Race cars will return on Modern Marvels.

295
00:23:38,621 --> 00:23:45,460
We now return to Race Cars on Modern Marvel.

296
00:23:49,180 --> 00:23:52,060
Stock car racing's popularity is soaring.

297
00:23:54,340 --> 00:23:57,900
Six million people attend NASCAR events each year.

298
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:02,640
And 150 million watch at home.

299
00:24:02,641 --> 00:24:10,280
Well-sponsored teams race cars that look like production vehicles, far different than Indy

300
00:24:10,281 --> 00:24:11,280
cars.

301
00:24:11,281 --> 00:24:16,760
People have always asked me over the years, what's the difference between driving an Indy

302
00:24:16,761 --> 00:24:18,820
car and a stock car?

303
00:24:18,821 --> 00:24:24,800
And I say, well, after driving an Indy car, getting in a stock car is like driving a three-bedroom

304
00:24:24,801 --> 00:24:26,760
house down the street.

305
00:24:26,761 --> 00:24:32,360
True, stock cars weigh more than 3,000 pounds, twice as much as an Indy car.

306
00:24:32,361 --> 00:24:34,780
But stock cars run almost as fast.

307
00:24:36,640 --> 00:24:41,500
The design of a stock car is completely different from an Indy car, and so is its history.

308
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:46,500
Stock car racing was born in the deep south.

309
00:24:47,860 --> 00:24:51,580
Economics prevented drivers from building expensive Indy-style vehicles.

310
00:24:51,581 --> 00:25:02,740
Instead, they found competition running slightly modified production cars on numerous local

311
00:25:02,741 --> 00:25:03,540
dirt tracks.

312
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:09,120
NASCAR really started in 1946 after World War II.

313
00:25:09,820 --> 00:25:16,300
A lot of the boys coming back from World War II that had led a cloistered life beforehand

314
00:25:16,301 --> 00:25:21,560
didn't necessarily want to go back to the farms or the cotton mills or furniture factories.

315
00:25:21,580 --> 00:25:24,580
so some of them want a little more zip out of life.

316
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:29,480
A driver might drive the same car in the race that he drove to the track.

317
00:25:30,060 --> 00:25:32,680
Off track, some drivers drove fast for a living.

318
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:40,000
And a lot of the early race car drivers of the early 50s and the late 40s

319
00:25:40,001 --> 00:25:46,960
were actually moonshiners that all moonshined by night and then raced on the weekend.

320
00:25:46,961 --> 00:25:56,860
An experienced race promoter, Bill France, established NASCAR, the National Association of Stock Car Auto Racing in 1947.

321
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:09,780
As rough and tumble as stock car racing was, he knew that it needed to be brought together under one banner, and that's what NASCAR was.

322
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:12,080
Quickly, crowds grew larger.

323
00:26:13,380 --> 00:26:16,120
The speedway was technicolor.

324
00:26:16,121 --> 00:26:21,680
You were off that day, and you paid $1.50 or $2 to go.

325
00:26:21,760 --> 00:26:22,780
It wasn't really expensive.

326
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:26,500
And you saw all these cars out there, and they were all kind of different colors.

327
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:28,560
One of them looked like an Easter egg.

328
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:31,460
And it was a show.

329
00:26:31,720 --> 00:26:33,260
It was a little bit of the fair.

330
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:36,060
It was a little bit of the circus.

331
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:38,780
And it was a lot of action.

332
00:26:39,560 --> 00:26:44,320
Soon, Super Speedways, larger-than-life personalities and big-time sponsorship,

333
00:26:44,321 --> 00:26:47,900
turned a rural interest into a national obsession.

334
00:26:48,920 --> 00:26:51,540
There's nothing stock on these cars anymore.

335
00:26:52,560 --> 00:26:54,220
Barring a few sheet metal pieces,

336
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:57,100
stock cars are carefully built from the ground up.

337
00:26:58,360 --> 00:27:01,560
Successful teams operate multi-million dollar facilities.

338
00:27:02,580 --> 00:27:05,080
At Joe Gibbs Racing near Charlotte, North Carolina,

339
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:07,220
they build everything by hand.

340
00:27:07,640 --> 00:27:09,140
Engine, body, and chassis.

341
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:13,140
It's a lot of work, considering they run 30 cars.

342
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:16,200
We have a couple of short track cars.

343
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:18,060
We have two road race cars.

344
00:27:18,480 --> 00:27:23,600
We have what we call our intermediate car, which is something like flat track.

345
00:27:23,940 --> 00:27:29,320
We'd run like it in Michigan and Indy to Charlotte, places like that.

346
00:27:30,400 --> 00:27:34,120
The cavern-like assembly room where all the pieces come together

347
00:27:34,121 --> 00:27:38,660
is nearly the length of a football field and spotless.

348
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:41,140
They're attentive to every detail.

349
00:27:41,141 --> 00:27:44,900
One small mistake could take a driver out of the race.

350
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:51,000
They start by altering a racing chassis to meet their specifications.

351
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:57,760
The tubular steel roll cage is extremely strong.

352
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:03,540
The whole chassis, everything from start to finish, is built to keep the driver safe,

353
00:28:03,860 --> 00:28:06,460
built for safety, built to keep the fans safe.

354
00:28:06,461 --> 00:28:13,460
Next, a machinist hand fabricates the body to resemble certain production automobiles.

355
00:28:13,461 --> 00:28:19,460
You know, they start off with a stock roof and hood and deck lid and they get them located

356
00:28:19,461 --> 00:28:24,460
and basically after that, everything you see except for the nose piece and the tail piece,

357
00:28:24,461 --> 00:28:29,460
which are fiberglass, everything else they make out of just a flat piece of sheet metal.

358
00:28:29,461 --> 00:28:34,460
The doors, the fenders, the quarter panels, the window tracks.

359
00:28:36,460 --> 00:28:41,460
Machinists use various templates to ensure the body meets NASCAR specifications.

360
00:28:41,461 --> 00:28:44,460
Then the car takes a trip to the paint booth.

361
00:28:44,461 --> 00:28:51,460
Next, the car applies its final cosmetic touches, powdering its nose piece with corporate sponsors.

362
00:28:51,461 --> 00:28:58,460
Nearby, engine builders manufacture eight-cylinder, 700-horsepower monsters.

363
00:28:58,461 --> 00:29:04,460
The motors that we use here really are more of what you could say a replica from stock.

364
00:29:04,461 --> 00:29:08,880
The parts are a derivative of the stock parts that originally came in vehicles,

365
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:13,280
but they have been modified either by the OEMs or by us to be used in racing,

366
00:29:13,380 --> 00:29:15,940
so they're more durable, can make more power,

367
00:29:16,040 --> 00:29:20,300
can withstand three and a half hours of absolute extreme use.

368
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,500
After assembly, an engineer checks motor performance

369
00:29:24,501 --> 00:29:26,820
and puts the engine into an awaiting car.

370
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,320
The team can test a car around the shot,

371
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:32,540
but until they actually hit the track,

372
00:29:32,541 --> 00:29:35,220
they won't know if the weeks of work have paid off.

373
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:39,620
Appropriately, that's determined in the heat of competition.

374
00:29:40,260 --> 00:29:41,640
A grueling competition.

375
00:29:42,300 --> 00:29:45,540
With races nearly every weekend for 10 months straight,

376
00:29:46,040 --> 00:29:48,440
drivers and teams push their cars to the limit.

377
00:29:49,120 --> 00:29:51,320
Racing, fighting, sweating, and swearing.

378
00:29:51,720 --> 00:29:54,100
And the fans wouldn't want it any other way.

379
00:29:55,480 --> 00:29:58,820
At the same time that stock car racing began in the South,

380
00:29:59,520 --> 00:30:04,520
In Southern California, home of drive-ins, hamburgers, and hot rods,

381
00:30:05,060 --> 00:30:07,360
a different style of racing was gaining momentum.

382
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:09,760
Next, dragsters.

383
00:30:12,660 --> 00:30:16,660
NASCAR actually slows cars down by mandating restrictor plates

384
00:30:16,760 --> 00:30:18,760
to cut the horsepower in the engines.

385
00:30:19,260 --> 00:30:21,680
Race cars will return on Modern Marvel.

386
00:30:25,080 --> 00:30:28,560
We now return to race cars on Modern Marvel.

387
00:30:28,820 --> 00:30:35,820
Automobile racing is synonymous with adrenaline, danger and high speeds.

388
00:30:35,821 --> 00:30:41,820
And nothing's faster than the nitromethane-burning fire belching top fuel dragsters.

389
00:30:41,821 --> 00:30:49,820
These land missiles go from 0 to 320 miles per hour in less than 5 seconds,

390
00:30:49,821 --> 00:30:53,820
covering a quarter mile in the blink of an eye.

391
00:30:53,821 --> 00:31:00,820
They're moving so fast it takes a parachute to slow them down.

392
00:31:00,821 --> 00:31:09,820
Dragsters and funny cars are built for a short sprint, not a 500-mile marathon like a stock or Indy car.

393
00:31:09,821 --> 00:31:13,820
Competing side by side, it's a simple elimination.

394
00:31:13,821 --> 00:31:17,820
You win, you stay. You lose, you're out.

395
00:31:17,821 --> 00:31:22,820
This continues until one car is ultimately victorious.

396
00:31:22,821 --> 00:31:28,100
Today's NHRA events draw huge crowds, but that's not the way it's always been.

397
00:31:32,660 --> 00:31:38,000
The sport of drag racing was born in Southern California in the late 1940s.

398
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:43,320
Young men listening to loud music started customizing their vehicles.

399
00:31:45,080 --> 00:31:51,720
America was in love with the automobile, and Hot Rodders, as they came to be known, built their cars for speed.

400
00:31:52,820 --> 00:32:01,780
For years, the dry lake beds 50 miles north of Los Angeles, California, provided a smooth,

401
00:32:01,781 --> 00:32:05,200
flat surface, excellent for amateur competition.

402
00:32:05,201 --> 00:32:10,820
It was a place where drivers could prove who was the best.

403
00:32:10,821 --> 00:32:17,460
But many hot rodders couldn't restrain themselves to weekend events, and the competition naturally

404
00:32:17,461 --> 00:32:18,980
bled onto the streets.

405
00:32:18,981 --> 00:32:31,200
When there was a common practice of people with roadsters and hot cars to meet at the local drive-in stand and show off their vehicles,

406
00:32:31,201 --> 00:32:35,640
and consequently the natural thing would be to challenge somebody for a race.

407
00:32:36,060 --> 00:32:38,783
And it became very typical there to say, you want to race?

408
00:32:38,784 --> 00:32:40,380
And the response was, drag it out.

409
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:43,384
Guys that I ran with, anyhow, we're all into hot rods.

410
00:32:43,385 --> 00:32:46,280
You know, we're in a car club, the road kings of Burbank,

411
00:32:46,281 --> 00:32:48,660
and, man, that's all we thought about.

412
00:32:48,780 --> 00:32:54,820
Cruise and Bob's drive in, and you'd go out in some street late at night on a side street,

413
00:32:55,160 --> 00:32:56,480
and we'd get it on.

414
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:58,780
You know, we'd have some doggone good drag races,

415
00:32:58,980 --> 00:33:01,200
and somebody would be in the middle of the street with a flag,

416
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:03,860
and you'd point to one car and point to the other car, and you'd rev it up.

417
00:33:04,380 --> 00:33:06,740
So, in fact, just like Rebel Without a Cause, you know,

418
00:33:06,800 --> 00:33:10,800
it was really the same type of thing, and see who had the fastest car.

419
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:14,060
Didn't you go back to Bob's when that was over with and talk about it?

420
00:33:14,061 --> 00:33:19,060
Not surprisingly, street racing proved dangerous and highly illegal.

421
00:33:19,061 --> 00:33:25,060
As most of society looked down on their behavior, the Hot Rodders were about to find a hero.

422
00:33:25,061 --> 00:33:29,060
When I think of drag racing, I think of Wally Parks.

423
00:33:29,061 --> 00:33:32,060
He's the man, he's the guy that started it.

424
00:33:32,061 --> 00:33:36,060
And he was really, more than anything, trying to organize a bunch of Hot Rodders,

425
00:33:36,061 --> 00:33:39,060
which I was one of, you know, to get them off the street

426
00:33:39,061 --> 00:33:46,060
the street and get them in a controlled environment where you can race these cars.

427
00:33:46,061 --> 00:33:53,060
In 1951, Wally Parks founded the National Hot Rod Association to organize hot rodders,

428
00:33:53,061 --> 00:33:56,849
eventually put on races and inspire safe racing.

429
00:33:56,850 --> 00:34:02,060
He also enlisted the help of an unlikely organization, the police.

430
00:34:02,061 --> 00:34:08,300
But the first commercially operated drag strip was in Santa Ana on the old Santa Ana Airport,

431
00:34:08,980 --> 00:34:09,680
where C.J.

432
00:34:09,681 --> 00:34:14,400
Hart, one of the pioneers, along with members of the California Highway Patrol,

433
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:20,200
put on the first race there to try to overcome the problems that they were having with street races in Orange County.

434
00:34:22,760 --> 00:34:26,280
The earliest dragsters were little more than a chassis and an engine.

435
00:34:26,281 --> 00:34:36,760
When the dragster first came into being, it simply was a stripped down, lightweight version of a roadster or an average automobile.

436
00:34:37,240 --> 00:34:39,400
And we can start out with one that's called the Bug.

437
00:34:39,920 --> 00:34:41,900
It was built by Dick Kraft.

438
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:46,740
It was the beginning, I guess, of what we call today's dragster.

439
00:34:48,420 --> 00:34:50,920
Most cars were junkyard specials.

440
00:34:51,300 --> 00:34:54,100
Recycled parts might have kept some of them from looking pretty.

441
00:34:54,101 --> 00:34:56,800
But it didn't keep them from running fast.

442
00:34:57,680 --> 00:35:02,680
By the mid-50s, drag racing was no longer exclusively a Southern California phenomenon.

443
00:35:04,120 --> 00:35:10,380
In 1955, the NHRA hosted their first national competition in Great Bend, Kansas.

444
00:35:11,080 --> 00:35:13,300
For racers from coast to coast.

445
00:35:15,040 --> 00:35:18,260
Over the next few decades, cars changed dramatically.

446
00:35:18,261 --> 00:35:28,160
When I first started driving race cars back in the late 60s, top fuel cars, the engine

447
00:35:28,161 --> 00:35:29,880
was located in front of the driver.

448
00:35:30,340 --> 00:35:34,980
It was a much shorter wheelbase car, and you sat over the rear end than the back of it.

449
00:35:35,260 --> 00:35:37,200
They're completely different today, obviously.

450
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:38,440
They're rear-engine dragsters.

451
00:35:38,540 --> 00:35:39,400
You sit in front.

452
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:41,960
They're much longer than we've ever anticipated.

453
00:35:42,140 --> 00:35:44,660
We never had wings on the cars in the early days.

454
00:35:44,661 --> 00:35:48,100
It was just a car without a wing, so we didn't have much downforce.

455
00:35:48,260 --> 00:35:54,700
Perhaps the biggest change to the dragster came with the introduction of nitromethane.

456
00:35:55,900 --> 00:36:04,640
This oxygen-rich fuel launched cars off the line and helped Kenny Bernstein break the 300-mile-per-hour barrier in 1992.

457
00:36:06,360 --> 00:36:09,060
You can't take it away. Nobody can.

458
00:36:09,780 --> 00:36:17,160
The way engines make horsepower is you put air and fuel into the chamber and you ignite it.

459
00:36:17,161 --> 00:36:24,160
The more air, the more fuel that you can put in there and achieve ignition, the faster and faster you go.

460
00:36:24,161 --> 00:36:29,160
Nitro methane as a fuel is really not that great of a fuel,

461
00:36:29,161 --> 00:36:37,160
but its characteristic of containing its own oxygen enables us to burn as much as 75 gallons per minute of it.

462
00:36:37,161 --> 00:36:43,160
So that tremendous volume with its own oxygen is why we can make these tremendous amounts of horsepower.

463
00:36:43,161 --> 00:36:47,200
Increased speeds also meant more dramatic accidents.

464
00:36:47,201 --> 00:36:51,200
But the NHRA's goal has always been better safety.

465
00:36:51,201 --> 00:36:55,240
I've been on fire. I've had a number of friends that were killed in car wrecks.

466
00:36:55,241 --> 00:36:59,240
And the safety that have been added to these

467
00:36:59,241 --> 00:37:03,280
cars have really improved in the last five to ten years. The NHRA safety

468
00:37:03,281 --> 00:37:07,280
safaris that they have, the groups that follow the circuit right there, these guys

469
00:37:07,281 --> 00:37:09,825
will jump in the roof of a car on fire and drag you out.

470
00:37:09,826 --> 00:37:11,280
They're some of my best friends.

471
00:37:11,281 --> 00:37:15,863
man, they saved my life a number of times, and I've been very lucky.

472
00:37:15,864 --> 00:37:17,480
Charismatic drivers such

473
00:37:17,481 --> 00:37:23,480
as Don Garlitz, Shirley Muldowney, and Don Prudhomme have brought the sport to a wider audience.

474
00:37:25,400 --> 00:37:30,560
Next, siblings to the dragsters, they're called funny cars, but they're nothing to laugh at.

475
00:37:31,500 --> 00:37:38,323
In 1950, the top recorded speed in a drag race was 120 miles per hour.

476
00:37:38,324 --> 00:37:40,760
In 1999, it was 330 miles

477
00:37:40,761 --> 00:37:43,760
Race Cars will return on Modern Marvels.

478
00:37:49,760 --> 00:37:53,760
We now return to Race Cars on Modern Marvels.

479
00:37:56,760 --> 00:38:01,760
Many early dragsters may have been chassis and engines rescued from the local junkyard.

480
00:38:02,760 --> 00:38:06,760
But today's team spent hundreds of thousands of dollars setting up a car

481
00:38:06,761 --> 00:38:11,360
setting up a car, and run motors generating more than 7,000 horsepower.

482
00:38:16,920 --> 00:38:21,220
Lighting one of these flightless rockets is literally like taking a ride on the shuttle.

483
00:38:22,900 --> 00:38:25,720
Drivers are subjected to more than 8 Gs.

484
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:31,860
Drag racing has covered a lot of quarter miles since the 1950s,

485
00:38:31,980 --> 00:38:36,680
and in the mid-1960s, the top fuel funny car was introduced to the sport.

486
00:38:36,760 --> 00:38:43,060
Donning a lightweight cosmetic body, the Funny Car is slightly different than the Top Fuel Dragster.

487
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:50,800
The placement of the engine and the length of the car also affect the way they both drive.

488
00:38:51,680 --> 00:38:56,680
You obviously sit in front of the engine in a dragster, so as a driver, you don't have a whole lot going on in front of you,

489
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:58,320
but trying to keep the car straight.

490
00:38:58,700 --> 00:39:03,500
The Funny Car, on the other hand, has got the engine in front of you, so there's more weight on the front end.

491
00:39:03,501 --> 00:39:06,760
it's much harder to drive, but you also have a body on you.

492
00:39:07,140 --> 00:39:09,820
And if you're claustrophobic, it's absolutely no place to be.

493
00:39:12,800 --> 00:39:18,220
Besides a talented driver, a good race also relies on the people behind the scenes,

494
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:22,120
the crew chiefs, mechanics, and machinists who build the cars.

495
00:39:24,140 --> 00:39:30,080
Here at John Force Racing, mechanics assemble cars in an impressive multi-million dollar facility.

496
00:39:30,081 --> 00:39:34,040
What we've got here are what we call our toy boxes.

497
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:43,080
We have this pneumatic control box here that has all of the air timers that control a lot of various functions on the race car.

498
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:49,340
It controls how fast the clutch locks up. It controls the fuel system.

499
00:39:52,040 --> 00:39:56,380
Dragsters and funny cars have two gears, high and reverse.

500
00:39:56,381 --> 00:39:59,700
Drivers don't shift when they race.

501
00:40:00,580 --> 00:40:06,680
Instead, a pre-programmed system controls the incremental amount the clutch engages the engine during a run.

502
00:40:07,820 --> 00:40:11,100
In the driver's compartment, we have the steering wheel.

503
00:40:11,220 --> 00:40:14,320
This little butterfly is all you need to guide it straight ahead.

504
00:40:14,640 --> 00:40:19,960
The kill switch right here is just used in an emergency to shut the engine off.

505
00:40:20,980 --> 00:40:24,920
The brake handle, this button deploys the fire bottles.

506
00:40:24,921 --> 00:40:31,920
We've got the gas pedal, the reverser lever, and the clutch pedal over there on the side.

507
00:40:31,921 --> 00:40:36,920
And that's all there is.

508
00:40:36,921 --> 00:40:39,920
Competition between teams is fierce.

509
00:40:39,921 --> 00:40:44,920
Members of the force team buy various engine components and assemble their own motors.

510
00:40:44,921 --> 00:40:48,920
Some parts they even manufacture themselves.

511
00:40:48,921 --> 00:40:52,920
We have a variety of software that allows us to design and

512
00:40:52,921 --> 00:40:56,920
engineer these parts on a computer before they ever go on a race car

513
00:40:56,921 --> 00:41:01,920
and then that software turns the parts into a reality on the CNC machines.

514
00:41:01,921 --> 00:41:09,920
The Computer Numerical Control, or CNC machine, whittles down solid blocks of aluminum into unique parts.

515
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:17,920
We take the concept and the design and turn it into something we can physically bolt on the car.

516
00:41:18,920 --> 00:41:25,620
Our ability to manufacture all that in-house gives us a technological advantage, we feel, over our competitors.

517
00:41:26,680 --> 00:41:32,860
The legendary Don the Snake Prudhomme's team runs both a funny car and a top fuel dragster.

518
00:41:34,840 --> 00:41:39,900
After the cars leave the shop and arrive at the track, the real work has just begun.

519
00:41:41,280 --> 00:41:44,680
Most of the work is done in the trailers, even on the road, believe it or not.

520
00:41:44,980 --> 00:41:46,420
It's kind of a rolling shop.

521
00:41:46,421 --> 00:41:51,460
And as you come into the trailer, you'll see several spare engines that we have down below here.

522
00:41:51,760 --> 00:41:58,840
Everything's loaded. Extra pistons, grinders, extra parts in here.

523
00:41:59,320 --> 00:42:02,360
Cabinets everywhere. More piston racks.

524
00:42:03,800 --> 00:42:05,780
I could pull out five or six different sets.

525
00:42:06,320 --> 00:42:09,540
The rolling shop is also a data analyzing center.

526
00:42:10,380 --> 00:42:14,480
After a run, the team downloads information from the car into a computer.

527
00:42:14,481 --> 00:42:21,480
A crew chief then studies everything from engine RPM to individual cylinder performance.

528
00:42:21,481 --> 00:42:27,480
And really all this goes back to how they want to set the car up for the next run.

529
00:42:27,481 --> 00:42:31,480
Between runs, the pits are a flurry of activity.

530
00:42:31,481 --> 00:42:36,480
A team completely breaks down and rebuilds the engine after every run.

531
00:42:36,481 --> 00:42:39,480
And they only have 75 minutes to do it.

532
00:42:39,481 --> 00:42:45,100
When a car gets back after a run, the first thing that the crew does is attack this car

533
00:42:45,101 --> 00:42:49,380
and take the engine completely apart down to the bare block and crankshaft.

534
00:42:49,381 --> 00:42:51,100
Heads come apart, everything comes out of it.

535
00:42:51,101 --> 00:42:55,820
Pistons come out of it, and they have it back together ready to fire up within an hour.

536
00:42:55,821 --> 00:42:57,240
It's really a sight to see.

537
00:42:57,241 --> 00:43:04,100
Whether behind the scenes or on the strip, drag racing puts on quite a show.

538
00:43:04,101 --> 00:43:06,280
And it's become an expensive show.

539
00:43:06,281 --> 00:43:16,088
You know the costs in running a fuel car in drag racing are considerable, but it costs about $4,000 every time you push that starter button and say we're going to try

540
00:43:16,089 --> 00:43:16,789
it again.

541
00:43:17,560 --> 00:43:21,720
The entire race car is pretty much a short-lived item.

542
00:43:23,420 --> 00:43:26,580
Rear tires are only good for four or five runs.

543
00:43:26,980 --> 00:43:32,000
An engine may be good for ten runs. It may only be good for one.

544
00:43:32,001 --> 00:43:36,100
and the nitromethane, you know, itself is $30 a gallon,

545
00:43:36,260 --> 00:43:38,800
and we go through about 14 gallons on a run.

546
00:43:39,320 --> 00:43:42,100
Without a bankroll, a car simply can't compete.

547
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:47,600
It's been said they introduced the funny car solely to provide space for sponsor logos.

548
00:43:48,220 --> 00:43:52,100
The real trick to this game isn't just how to win a championship or give an interview.

549
00:43:52,460 --> 00:43:54,920
It's how to keep the sponsor happy. You've got to keep selling their product.

550
00:43:55,280 --> 00:43:57,220
These cars are, like I said, moving billboards.

551
00:43:57,980 --> 00:43:59,740
Without corporate America, you're history.

552
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:06,200
And that's what drag racing has in common with other types of racing.

553
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:10,180
Big expense equals the need for big-time sponsorship.

554
00:44:11,180 --> 00:44:14,280
But that's not all the various types of racing share.

555
00:44:14,840 --> 00:44:17,480
There's also the competitive nature of the drivers.

556
00:44:18,360 --> 00:44:21,080
Best race drivers can drive anything.

557
00:44:21,880 --> 00:44:26,640
And I think the one thing that they all have in common is that they're competitors.

558
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:30,800
It's the cars that people see from the grandstands.

559
00:44:30,801 --> 00:44:35,560
But a race is so much more than a collection of nuts, bolts, and bearings circling the track.

560
00:44:37,560 --> 00:44:46,720
For the drivers, it's about wrangling sponsors, recapturing you, cheating death, and seizing the checkered flag.

